<body>

<header class="masthead">
  <div class="kicker">MSN and DNP Practice Improvement Projects</div>
  <h1>Statistical Test Selector for EBP and QI Projects</h1>
  <p>A guided decision aid that walks you through your project, defines terms along the way, and points you to the simplest defensible design and analysis.</p>
</header>

<div class="notice">
  <strong>Read this first.</strong>
  <p>EBP and QI projects are local practice improvement work, not generalizable research. The goal is to improve care in one setting, not to produce findings meant to apply everywhere. That is why most of these projects are determined non-research or IRB exempt, and why the analysis should stay as simple as the question allows. Choose the plainest method that answers your aim. Often a clear set of descriptive statistics, or a run chart, is enough.</p>
</div>

<nav class="tabs">
  <button class="active" data-tab="tool">Guided Tool</button>
  <button data-tab="matrix">Reference Matrix</button>
  <button data-tab="glossary">Glossary</button>
  <button data-tab="about">About and References</button>
</nav>

<div class="wrap">

  <!-- ============ GUIDED TOOL ============ -->
  <section class="panel active" id="tool">
    <div id="crumbs" class="crumbs"></div>
    <div id="steps"></div>
    <div class="btn-row" style="margin-top:4px">
      <button class="btn ghost" id="restart">Start over</button>
      <button class="btn ghost" id="back" style="display:none">Back one step</button>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ MATRIX ============ -->
  <section class="panel" id="matrix">
    <div class="card">
      <span class="step-label">Quick reference</span>
      <h2>Test selection matrix</h2>
      <p class="lead">Find the row for your outcome measure, then the column for how your project is structured. The bold entry is the simplest defensible test. The lighter entry is the alternative to use when a continuous outcome is markedly skewed or your sample is small (roughly under 30), or when your outcome is ordinal.</p>
      <div class="tablewrap">
        <table>
          <caption>Comparing outcomes before and after, or between groups</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Outcome measure (your dependent variable)</th>
              <th>One group, before vs after (paired)</th>
              <th>Two separate groups</th>
              <th>Three or more time points or groups</th>
              <th>Compare to a fixed target or benchmark</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th>Continuous<br><span style="font-weight:400;font-size:12.5px;color:var(--ink-soft)">e.g., HbA1c, blood pressure, weight, length of stay, a summed scale score</span></th>
              <td><span class="test">Paired-samples t test</span><span class="alt">Skewed or small n: Wilcoxon signed-rank test</span></td>
              <td><span class="test">Independent-samples t test</span><span class="alt">Skewed or small n: Mann-Whitney U test</span></td>
              <td><span class="test">One-way ANOVA (groups) or repeated-measures ANOVA (time points)</span><span class="alt">Skewed or small n: Kruskal-Wallis or Friedman test</span></td>
              <td><span class="test">One-sample t test</span></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th>Categorical, two outcomes<br><span style="font-weight:400;font-size:12.5px;color:var(--ink-soft)">e.g., compliant yes or no, readmitted yes or no, fall yes or no</span></th>
              <td><span class="test">McNemar test</span><span class="alt">Same patients counted as yes or no at two times</span></td>
              <td><span class="test">Chi-square test of independence</span><span class="alt">Small expected counts (any cell under 5): Fisher exact test</span></td>
              <td><span class="test">Chi-square test</span></td>
              <td><span class="test">One-sample proportion test or binomial test</span></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th>Ordinal<br><span style="font-weight:400;font-size:12.5px;color:var(--ink-soft)">e.g., a single Likert item, satisfaction 1 to 5, pain stage</span></th>
              <td><span class="test">Wilcoxon signed-rank test</span></td>
              <td><span class="test">Mann-Whitney U test</span></td>
              <td><span class="test">Kruskal-Wallis (groups) or Friedman (time points)</span></td>
              <td><span class="test">Compare to target with descriptive medians, sign test if needed</span></td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <div class="tablewrap">
        <table>
          <caption>Tracking a measure over many time points (the usual choice for QI)</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Situation</th><th>Recommended display</th><th>What it tells you</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th>Just starting, few data points</th>
              <td><span class="test">Run chart</span><span class="alt">Plot each point over time against a median centerline</span></td>
              <td>Whether a real shift, trend, or run has appeared, using probability-based rules, without needing many points</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th>Roughly 15 to 20 or more time points</th>
              <td><span class="test">Control chart (SPC or Shewhart chart)</span><span class="alt">p-chart for a proportion, c-chart or u-chart for counts and rates, I-chart for individual continuous values</span></td>
              <td>Whether variation is normal background noise (common cause) or a real signal tied to your change (special cause), using control limits</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="warnbox" style="margin-top:18px"><b>Why charts often beat a single before-and-after test in QI.</b> A run chart or control chart shows whether your improvement actually held over time and separates a true change from ordinary fluctuation. A one-time pre and post test cannot do either. Many strong QI projects report no p value at all.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <div class="tablewrap">
        <table>
          <caption>Choosing a design for a practice project</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Design</th><th>What it means</th><th>Typical analysis</th><th>Research or non-research</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th>Descriptive</th>
              <td>Observe and summarize one group, no comparison group and no manipulation</td>
              <td>Descriptive statistics, frequencies, simple charts</td>
              <td>Almost always non-research</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th>One-group pretest-posttest (pre-experimental)</th>
              <td>One group measured before and after a change, no comparison group. The most common practice-project design</td>
              <td>Paired t test, Wilcoxon signed-rank, or McNemar, depending on the outcome type</td>
              <td>Usually non-research or exempt</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th>Interrupted time series (quasi-experimental)</th>
              <td>The outcome measured repeatedly before and after a change, watching for a sustained shift</td>
              <td>Run chart, then control chart as data accumulate</td>
              <td>Usually non-research, well suited to QI</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th>Nonequivalent comparison group (quasi-experimental)</th>
              <td>An intervention group compared with a separate group that was not randomly assigned</td>
              <td>Independent-samples t test, Mann-Whitney, or chi-square, depending on the outcome type</td>
              <td>Often non-research, but confirm with your IRB</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th>Experimental (randomized controlled trial)</th>
              <td>Random assignment to intervention or comparison, plus a control group</td>
              <td>The same comparison tests, with randomization supporting stronger causal claims</td>
              <td>Usually research, requires full IRB review</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="warnbox" style="margin-top:18px;background:var(--teal-tint);border-color:#bcd9d5;border-left-color:var(--teal);color:var(--ink-soft)"><b>Reading the table.</b> Designs near the top are simpler and almost always non-research. As you move down, the design gets stronger but also more demanding, and randomization at the bottom usually tips a project into research. For most EBP and QI work, one of the first three rows is the right home.</div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ GLOSSARY ============ -->
  <section class="panel" id="glossary">
    <div class="card glossary">
      <span class="step-label">Plain-language definitions</span>
      <h2>Glossary</h2>
      <dl>
        <dt>EBP, QI, and research</dt>
        <dd>Evidence-based practice puts existing best evidence into local practice. Quality improvement uses data to make immediate improvements to a local process or outcome. Research is a systematic investigation designed to create knowledge meant to generalize beyond your setting. Only the last one routinely requires full IRB review. The intent to publish does not by itself turn a QI project into research, and the final determination is made by your IRB or institution, not by you.</dd>

        <dt>Study designs, from simplest to strongest</dt>
        <dd>A descriptive design summarizes one group with no comparison. A one-group pretest-posttest design (pre-experimental) measures one group before and after a change, which is simple but cannot rule out other causes. A quasi-experimental design adds a comparison, either a separate nonrandomized group or repeated measures over time (interrupted time series), without random assignment. A true experiment (randomized controlled trial) adds random assignment and a control group, which strengthens causal claims but usually makes the project research. Practice projects almost always sit in the first three.</dd>

        <dt>Dependent and independent variable</dt>
        <dd>Your dependent variable is the outcome you measure, such as fall rate or HbA1c. Your independent variable is what you think influences it, usually your intervention or group. The data type of your dependent variable drives most of the test choice.</dd>

        <dt>Levels of measurement</dt>
        <dd>Continuous data are numbers on a real scale where the distance between values is meaningful, such as weight or blood pressure. Categorical data sort cases into named groups with no order, such as yes or no. Ordinal data have an order but uneven or unknown spacing, such as a 1 to 5 satisfaction rating.</dd>

        <dt>Paired versus independent</dt>
        <dd>Paired data come from the same people measured twice, such as before and after, so each before value links to one after value. Independent data come from two separate sets of people, such as a unit that got the intervention and a unit that did not.</dd>

        <dt>Parametric versus nonparametric</dt>
        <dd>Parametric tests, such as the t test, assume the outcome is continuous and reasonably normally distributed, and they compare means. Nonparametric tests, such as Wilcoxon or Mann-Whitney, make fewer assumptions, compare ranks or medians, and are the safer choice with small samples or skewed data.</dd>

        <dt>Descriptive versus inferential statistics</dt>
        <dd>Descriptive statistics summarize what happened in your sample, such as means, percentages, and frequencies. Inferential statistics, such as t tests and chi-square, estimate whether a difference is likely more than chance. Many EBP and QI projects are answered well by descriptive statistics alone.</dd>

        <dt>Common cause and special cause variation</dt>
        <dd>Common cause variation is the normal up and down noise present in any stable process. Special cause variation is a signal that something real changed. Run charts and control charts exist to tell these two apart.</dd>

        <dt>p value, confidence interval, and effect size</dt>
        <dd>A p value estimates how surprising your result would be if nothing really changed. A confidence interval gives a plausible range for the true difference. An effect size, such as Cohen d, says how large the difference is in practical terms. With small samples, report the confidence interval and effect size, not just the p value, because a real and useful change can still miss statistical significance.</dd>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ ABOUT ============ -->
  <section class="panel" id="about">
    <div class="card about">
      <span class="step-label">How to use this aid</span>
      <h2>About this tool</h2>
      <p>This aid is for you as you plan the analysis for your MSN or DNP practice improvement project. It deliberately steers toward the simplest defensible method, because these projects answer focused local questions, usually with modest sample sizes. Use it to think through your design and analysis. It is a starting point, not a substitute for your own judgment, your statistics resources, your project chair, or your institution's determination process. Every underlined term carries a plain-language definition, so tap or hover anything you do not remember.</p>

      <h3>Three things to keep central</h3>
      <p>First, confirm your project is non-research or exempt before you worry about tests, because your design follows from that. Second, define your outcome variable and its data type clearly, since that single decision drives most of the test choice. Third, prefer descriptive summaries and time-series charts when they answer your aim, and reserve inferential tests for when a genuine comparison is the point.</p>

      <h3>References</h3>
      <div class="refs">
